.TH CLOGGER 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
clogger \- overview of the structured logger
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The module writes structured log records.  A record is a sequence of
key=value pairs, in this order:
  - A fixed header that gives the time, the level, the process, the
    thread and the source location.
  - The persistent fields of the logger.
  - The message.
The module writes one of three formats: logfmt (the default), NDJSON
and RFC 5424 syslog.  See clog_set_format(3).

A logger writes to one of these outputs:
  - A file descriptor that the caller owns (clog_open_fd_mp(3)).
  - A file that the logger opens itself (clog_open_file_mp(3)).  This
    output has optional size rotation, time rotation and gzip
    compression of rotated files.
Each kind of logger can log synchronously.  It can also give the write
to its own writer thread (async mode, through a clog_async_cfg_t).
.fi
.SS Handles
.nf
A clog is an opaque value handle, a uint64_t.  It is not a pointer.
Do not cast it to void * or from void *.  CLOG_INVALID, which is 0,
means "no logger".  The open functions and clog_derive(3) give
CLOG_INVALID on failure.  Both if (!lg) and lg == CLOG_INVALID test
for it.

Each function that takes a handle needs a live handle.  These handles
stop the process with a diagnostic:
  - CLOG_INVALID.
  - A handle whose logger is closed.
  - A stale handle.
The library never ignores such a handle silently.

clog_derive(3) makes a second handle on the same output.  A root logger
and its derived loggers share these items:
  - The output target, the format and the syslog facility.
  - The rotation.
  - The async writer thread.
  - The allocator.
  - The lock that serialises the writes.
Each handle has its own minimum level and its own fields.
.fi
.SS Levels
.nf
  CLOG_TRACE  0  The finest detail.
  CLOG_DEBUG  1
  CLOG_INFO   2  The usual minimum in production.
  CLOG_WARN   3
  CLOG_ERROR  4  Adds a backtrace.
  CLOG_ALERT  5  An operator must act immediately (RFC 5424 severity
                 1).  Adds a backtrace.
  CLOG_FATAL  6  Adds a backtrace, then stops the process with
                 exit(EXIT_FAILURE).  Ignores the minimum level.
  CLOG_OFF    7  As a minimum level, stops all output except
                 CLOG_FATAL.

The logger silently drops a record below its minimum level.  Each of
the ccol_log_trace(3) to ccol_log_fatal(3) macros logs at one level.
clog_set_level(3) changes the minimum at any time.
.fi
.SS Record contents
.nf
The proc key has the form <name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>).  The first
group is the basename of the executable and the process ID.  The
second group is the name and the ID of the thread that logged.
ccol_set_thread_name(3) gives a new name to a thread.  The records of
that thread then show the new name.

In logfmt, the logger writes a field value or a message in double
quotes when it holds a space, an '=', a '"', a backslash or a control
character.  A backslash escapes those characters (\e", \e\e, \en, \er,
\et or \exNN).  Therefore, a message or a value can never split a
record across lines.  JSON and syslog use their own escapes; see
clog_set_format(3) and clog_set_field(3).

A record has a hard size limit of 16 MiB.  The library never writes a
truncated or malformed record:
  - The library replaces a record above the limit with a well-formed
    record in the same format.  That record keeps the header and says
    "log record too large to emit".
  - An allocation failure while the library makes a record gives a
    record that says "log record dropped".
Therefore, a record never disappears without a trace.  A "log record
truncated: ..." record at CLOG_ERROR identifies the records that a
write could not deliver; see clog_open_fd_mp(3).
.fi
.SS Backtraces
.nf
ccol_log_error(3), ccol_log_alert(3) and ccol_log_fatal(3) add a
backtrace:
  - In logfmt: continuation lines with a tab indent.
  - In JSON: a "bt" array.
  - In syslog: one message for each frame.
For symbol names, link the program with -rdynamic.  The capture works
on platforms with execinfo.h (glibc, FreeBSD, macOS).  When the capture
fails, a marker replaces the frames: "#error backtrace unavailable" in
logfmt, and a "bt_error" key in JSON.  Therefore, a missing backtrace
is always visible.
.fi
.SS Memory management
.nf
clog_open_fd_mp(3) and clog_open_file_mp(3) take an optional
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t.  The logger makes these allocations through it,
for the root logger and for each logger derived from it:
  - The shared output state.
  - The write buffers.
  - The message buffers.
  - The fields.
The one exception is the symbol text of a backtrace.
backtrace_symbols(3) allocates that text with malloc(3).  All four
function pointers must be set.  The library copies the struct.
Therefore, the caller can free it after the open.
.fi
.SS Thread safety
.nf
You can call each function from many threads at the same time on
handles that stay open.  One lock serialises the writes and the
rotation of one output target.  Therefore, the lines of two threads
never mix, and a backtrace always follows its own record.  The library
synchronises the level and the fields of a logger separately from that
lock.

clog_close(3) is the exception, as for all handle types of the
library.  While it runs, no other thread can use or close the same
handle.  You can close one handle while a sibling handle (the root or
a different derived logger) logs.
.fi
.SS Process exit
.nf
The library registers an exit handler when it loads.  When the process
ends with exit(3) or a return from main(), this handler does two
things, within a budget of 5 seconds:
  - It writes the records that each async logger holds.
  - It completes the queued compressions.
ccol_log_fatal(3) uses the same drain.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3)
run no exit handler.  If the queued records are important, call
clog_flush(3) or clog_close(3) first.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3) for the
details.
.fi
.SS fork(2)
.nf
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with this module:
  - Call fork(2) before you open a logger.
  - Open a logger, and then call fork(2) and exec(3) immediately.
A child that continues with no exec(3) while a logger from before the
fork is live is not supported.

As a defense in depth, an inherited handle works in such a child.  An
async logger in the child changes to synchronous writes, because its
writer thread does not exist in the child.  This fallback is part of
the fork protection of the library.  A build with
-DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 does not include it.  A process from such
a build must not fork while it holds a live async logger.
.fi
.SS SIGPIPE
.nf
A write to a pipe or a socket whose reader is gone causes SIGPIPE.  By
default, the library prevents the end of the process from that signal.
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3) describes how.  It also describes the policy
that leaves SIGPIPE to the application.
.fi
